content:
I maintain a research notebook in Google Docs. It has eleven tabs nested three levels deep, and I edit it every day. I wanted Claude to edit it directly instead of me copy-pasting between a chat window and a browser tab.

The official Google Drive connector could not do it. This post is the full path I took to a working setup, including the dead ends and every script.

**What you get at the end:** Claude can read any tab of your document, append to a specific tab, and issue arbitrary Google Docs API `batchUpdate` requests (headings, styles, tables, section breaks) — all scoped to one document, addressed by tab ID, behind a shared secret.

**Time:** about 45 minutes if nothing goes wrong. Budget an hour.

---

## How to use this post

This guide is written so you can paste the whole thing into Claude and have it walk you through setup interactively. If you want to do that, paste the post and then say:

> I want to set this up for my own Google Doc. Walk me through it one step at a time, wait for me to report the result of each step before moving on, and adapt to my OS and environment. Start with the prerequisites check.

Claude will have the full script set, the verification commands, and the known failure modes. The "Gotchas" section near the end exists specifically so your assistant can recognize an error message and know what it means.

If you'd rather just do it yourself, read straight through.

---

## The problem

Claude's Google Drive connector can read Docs fine. It cannot write their contents.

The `update_file` tool only accepts `title` and `parent_id` — its own documentation says "currently only title and parent_id are supported." `create_file` makes new files but takes no file ID, so it can't overwrite an existing one. There is no `batchUpdate` anywhere in the connector's surface.

So: reading is solved, writing is not. Editing an existing Doc's content meant browser automation — slow, screenshot-driven, and unreliable on a document with a complex tab structure.

ChatGPT's Google Drive integration does support full content editing, because it's a first-party integration that calls the Docs API directly. That's the gap I set out to close.

---

## Two candidate paths

### Path A — a custom OAuth client

Register an OAuth 2.0 client in Google Cloud Console, request the `documents` scope, run a local MCP server that calls the Docs REST API directly.

This is the standard approach and it's clean. The risk: if your account is in a Google Workspace organization (a university, a company), an administrator can block third-party OAuth applications. You don't find out until you try.

### Path B — Google Apps Script as the execution layer

Write an Apps Script bound to your account that does the document manipulation, deploy it as a web app, and have a thin local MCP server call it over HTTP.

I initially assumed Apps Script would bypass OAuth restrictions entirely because the script runs under your own account. **That assumption is wrong.** Apps Script still requires you to grant OAuth scopes, and admins have explicit controls over Apps Script authorization, can disable Apps Script org-wide, can disable individual script projects, and can restrict `UrlFetchApp` outbound domains.

What is plausibly true — and was true in my case — is that an organization may allowlist trusted third-party apps while blocking unknown ones, and separately permit self-authored scripts. Different app identity, different policy outcome. But that's an empirical question about your organization, not a general rule.

### Which to try first

**Test Path B's authorization first**, because it's dramatically cheaper to test: no Cloud project, no consent screen, no client ID. Open the Apps Script editor, paste five lines, click Run, see whether the authorization dialog completes. Two minutes.

Both paths die at the same gate — can your account grant Docs scopes to anything at all — so the cheap test is also the informative one.

I ended up staying on Path B after it passed, because the authorization was already proven and it avoids consent screen configuration, Google verification, and OAuth token lifecycle management entirely.

---

## Architecture

```
Claude Desktop
      │
      │ stdio (MCP protocol)
      ▼
Local Node.js MCP server
      │
      │ HTTPS POST  { secret, op, args }
      ▼
Apps Script Web App  (runs as you, on Google's infrastructure)
      │
      │ Advanced Docs Service → documents.batchUpdate
      ▼
Your Google Doc
```

The Node process holds no Google credentials. All Google authorization lives in the Apps Script project, which was authorized once, interactively, by you. The only secret on the client side is a shared string that authenticates the caller to your script.

---

## Prerequisites

- A Google account that can access the target document
- Node.js 18 or newer (`node --version`)
- Claude Desktop
- The document you want to edit, open in a browser

---

## Step 1 — Verify your account can authorize Apps Script

This is the gate. Everything downstream depends on it, and it costs two minutes.

**1.1** Get your document ID from the URL:

```
https://docs.google.com/document/d/1AbCdEfGhIjKlMnOpQrStUvWxYz0123456789/edit?tab=t.0
                                  └────────────── document ID ──────────────┘
```

It's the segment between `/d/` and `/edit`.

**1.2** Go to [script.google.com](https://script.google.com).

**Check the account avatar in the top right.** If you're signed into multiple Google accounts, the editor may open under the wrong one, and the test result will be meaningless. Use a private window signed into only the target account if you're unsure.

**1.3** Click **New project**. Delete the default `myFunction` stub and paste:

```javascript
function testDocsAccess() {
  const doc = DocumentApp.openById('YOUR_DOCUMENT_ID');
  Logger.log('Title: ' + doc.getName());
  const tabs = doc.getTabs();
  Logger.log('Top-level tabs: ' + tabs.length);
  tabs.forEach(t => Logger.log(' - ' + t.getTitle()));
}
```

**1.4** Save (Ctrl+S / Cmd+S), give the project a name, then click **Run**.

**1.5** An authorization dialog appears. This is the moment that matters.

- "Authorization required" → **Review permissions**
- Choose your account
- You may see "Google hasn't verified this app" → **Advanced** → **Go to [project name] (unsafe)**. This is your own script; the warning is expected.
- Review the requested scopes → **Allow**

**1.6** Check the Execution log at the bottom.

**If it printed your document title and tab list:** the gate is passed. Continue.

**If you got `admin_policy_enforced`, "This app is blocked", or "administrator has disabled":** your organization blocks this. Stop here — switching to a different Cloud project or publishing mode will not help, because the restriction is on what your *account* may authorize, not on who owns the project. Contact your IT administrator with the error text and the scopes you requested.

---

## Step 2 — Map the tab structure

`getTabs()` only returns top-level tabs. If your document has nested tabs, you need the full tree, and you need the tab IDs — every write operation from here on addresses a tab explicitly.

Add this function and run it:

```javascript
function listAllTabs() {
  const doc = DocumentApp.openById('YOUR_DOCUMENT_ID');
  function walk(tabs, depth) {
    tabs.forEach(t => {
      Logger.log('  '.repeat(depth) + '- ' + t.getTitle() + '  [' + t.getId() + ']');
      const kids = t.getChildTabs();
      if (kids && kids.length) walk(kids, depth + 1);
    });
  }
  walk(doc.getTabs(), 0);
}
```

Output looks like:

```
- Project Overview          [t.svm8bnpde0th]
  - Decision Log            [t.q8vlzhwnz24q]
  - Analysis Plan           [t.kjji601dh5uq]
- Data Preparation          [t.aq1k8vxg49yt]
  - Questionnaire & Linkage [t.mcvpkd35oiqa]
- References                [t.8lvmuqnuhhd0]
```

Keep this output. You'll want it when telling Claude which tab to write to.

> **This matters more than it looks.** In a multi-tab document, `doc.getBody()` operates on the *active* tab, or the first tab if none is active — not the whole document. A naive implementation that calls `getBody()` will silently write everything into tab one. Every operation in this guide takes an explicit `tabId` for exactly this reason.

---

## Step 3 — Deploy as a web app and verify anonymous access

The MCP server is a plain Node process with no Google session, so the endpoint has to be callable without Google authentication.

**3.1** Add a trivial GET handler to the script:

```javascript
function doGet(e) {
  return ContentService
    .createTextOutput(JSON.stringify({ ok: true, ts: new Date().toISOString() }))
    .setMimeType(ContentService.MimeType.JSON);
}
```

Save.

**3.2** **Deploy** → **New deployment** → gear icon → **Web app**

- **Execute as:** Me
- **Who has access:** Anyone

Deploy, and copy the `/exec` URL.

> **"Execute as: Me" controls the execution identity, not who may call it.** Of the access levels, only the fully anonymous one is callable by a plain HTTP client. The others still require a Google-authenticated caller, which a Node process does not have.

**3.3** Verify with a session-free request. **Do not test this by opening the URL in your normal browser** — you're signed into Google there, so success proves nothing.

```bash
curl -s -i "YOUR_WEB_APP_URL"
```

Read the `Location` header in the 302 response:

| Location points to | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `script.googleusercontent.com/macros/echo?...` | Anonymous access works. Continue. |
| `accounts.google.com/...` | Authentication required. Go back and set access to Anyone. |

Follow the redirect (`curl -s -L`) to see the JSON body itself.

---

## Step 4 — The write layer

Now replace the whole script with the real implementation.

**4.1 Generate a shared secret.**

macOS / Linux:
```bash
openssl rand -base64 36 | tr -d '/+=' | cut -c1-48
```

Windows PowerShell:
```powershell
-join ((48..57)+(65..90)+(97..122) | Get-Random -Count 48 | % {[char]$_})
```

**4.2 Enable the Advanced Docs Service.** In the Apps Script editor, click **+** next to **Services**, find **Google Docs API**, click **Add**.

Without this, `Docs.Documents.batchUpdate` throws `Docs is not defined`. This is easy to forget and the error is only visible at call time.

**4.3 Replace the entire script contents:**

```javascript
/** ===== CONFIG ===== */
const DOC_ID = 'YOUR_DOCUMENT_ID';
const SECRET = 'YOUR_GENERATED_SECRET';

/** ===== ENTRY ===== */
function doPost(e) {
  let req;
  try { req = JSON.parse(e.postData.contents); }
  catch (err) { return json({ ok: false, error: 'bad JSON' }); }

  if (req.secret !== SECRET) return json({ ok: false, error: 'unauthorized' });

  try {
    switch (req.op) {
      case 'listTabs':    return json({ ok: true, tabs: listTabs_() });
      case 'readTab':     return json({ ok: true, text: readTab_(req.tabId) });
      case 'appendText':  return json({ ok: true, result: appendText_(req.tabId, req.text) });
      case 'batchUpdate': return json({ ok: true, result: batchUpdate_(req.requests) });
      default:            return json({ ok: false, error: 'unknown op: ' + req.op });
    }
  } catch (err) {
    return json({ ok: false, error: String(err) });
  }
}

function doGet(e) {
  return json({ ok: true, ts: new Date().toISOString(), note: 'use POST' });
}

/** ===== OPS ===== */
function listTabs_() {
  const doc = DocumentApp.openById(DOC_ID);
  const out = [];
  (function walk(tabs, depth) {
    tabs.forEach(function (t) {
      out.push({ id: t.getId(), title: t.getTitle(), depth: depth });
      const kids = t.getChildTabs();
      if (kids && kids.length) walk(kids, depth + 1);
    });
  })(doc.getTabs(), 0);
  return out;
}

function readTab_(tabId) {
  const tab = DocumentApp.openById(DOC_ID).getTab(tabId);
  if (!tab) throw new Error('tab not found: ' + tabId);
  return tab.asDocumentTab().getBody().getText();
}

function appendText_(tabId, text) {
  const doc = DocumentApp.openById(DOC_ID);
  const tab = doc.getTab(tabId);
  if (!tab) throw new Error('tab not found: ' + tabId);
  const body = tab.asDocumentTab().getBody();
  const lines = String(text).split('\n');
  lines.forEach(function (line) { body.appendParagraph(line); });
  doc.saveAndClose();
  return { appended: lines.length };
}

function batchUpdate_(requests) {
  if (!Array.isArray(requests) || !requests.length) {
    throw new Error('requests must be a non-empty array');
  }
  return Docs.Documents.batchUpdate({ requests: requests }, DOC_ID);
}

/** ===== UTIL ===== */
function json(obj) {
  return ContentService.createTextOutput(JSON.stringify(obj))
    .setMimeType(ContentService.MimeType.JSON);
}
```

Two design decisions worth calling out:

**Every operation requires an explicit `tabId`.** There is no implicit "current tab." This is what prevents the `getBody()` failure mode described in Step 2.

**`batchUpdate` is a raw passthrough.** `DocumentApp` cannot create, delete, or modify tabs, has no section-break control, offers no atomic multi-request commit, and no revision-based concurrency control. The Docs REST API has all of these. Rather than choosing between the convenient API and the capable one, the passthrough gives you both — simple operations use `appendText`, anything involving real formatting goes through `batchUpdate`. The document allowlist and shared secret contain the risk of exposing a general-purpose endpoint.

**4.4 Save, then redeploy.**

**Deploy** → **Manage deployments** → pencil icon → **Version: New version** → **Deploy**

> **Saving is not deploying.** A deployment is a snapshot of the saved code at deploy time. Editing the script and saving changes nothing about what the live URL serves until you push a new version. Editing an existing deployment keeps the same URL; creating a new deployment gives you a different one.

**4.5 Test the full surface.**

Run all four calls. The first is read-only; the rest write to a tab you should pick specifically for testing.

### macOS / Linux

Set your values once:

```bash
URL='YOUR_WEB_APP_URL'
SEC='YOUR_SECRET'
TAB='YOUR_TAB_ID'
```

Heredocs keep the JSON readable and let you interpolate the shell variables. Pipe through `jq` if you have it, or `python3 -m json.tool`, for readable output.

```bash
# Read-only: list tabs
curl -s -L -X POST "$URL" \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d @- <<EOF | jq
{"secret":"$SEC","op":"listTabs"}
EOF
```

```bash
# Write: append to a specific tab
curl -s -L -X POST "$URL" \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d @- <<EOF | jq
{"secret":"$SEC","op":"appendText","tabId":"$TAB","text":"[test] appendText OK"}
EOF
```

```bash
# Write: raw Docs API batchUpdate
curl -s -L -X POST "$URL" \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d @- <<EOF | jq
{
  "secret": "$SEC",
  "op": "batchUpdate",
  "requests": [
    {
      "insertText": {
        "endOfSegmentLocation": { "tabId": "$TAB" },
        "text": "\n[test] batchUpdate OK\n"
      }
    }
  ]
}
EOF
```

```bash
# Non-ASCII content
curl -s -L -X POST "$URL" \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8' \
  -d @- <<EOF | jq
{"secret":"$SEC","op":"appendText","tabId":"$TAB","text":"[test] 中文 / émoji / ünïcode"}
EOF
```

Three notes on the bash version:

- **Use an unquoted heredoc delimiter** (`<<EOF`, not `<<'EOF'`) so `$SEC` and `$TAB` expand. The `\n` sequences pass through untouched — bash only treats `$`, `` ` ``, `\`, and newline as special inside a heredoc — so they reach the JSON parser as proper string escapes.
- **Keep the default `-L` redirect behavior.** curl converts POST to GET when following a 302, which is correct here: Apps Script already executed `doPost` before redirecting, and the redirect target just serves the result. Do not add `--post302`.
- **UTF-8 needs no special handling.** Modern shells and curl send UTF-8 by default.

### Windows PowerShell

PowerShell needs more care, and the failures are quiet rather than loud.

```powershell
$url = 'YOUR_WEB_APP_URL'
$sec = 'YOUR_SECRET'
$tab = 'YOUR_TAB_ID'

# Read-only: list tabs
$body = @{ secret = $sec; op = 'listTabs' } | ConvertTo-Json
Invoke-RestMethod -Uri $url -Method Post -ContentType 'application/json' -Body $body
```

```powershell
# Write: append to a specific tab
$body = @{
  secret = $sec
  op     = 'appendText'
  tabId  = $tab
  text   = '[test] appendText OK'
} | ConvertTo-Json

Invoke-RestMethod -Uri $url -Method Post -ContentType 'application/json' -Body $body
```

```powershell
# Write: raw Docs API batchUpdate
$req = @{
  insertText = @{
    endOfSegmentLocation = @{ tabId = $tab }
    text = "`n[test] batchUpdate OK`n"
  }
}

$body = @{ secret = $sec; op = 'batchUpdate'; requests = @($req) } | ConvertTo-Json -Depth 10
Invoke-RestMethod -Uri $url -Method Post -ContentType 'application/json' -Body $body
```

```powershell
# Non-ASCII content — note the explicit UTF-8 encoding
$body = @{
  secret = $sec
  op     = 'appendText'
  tabId  = $tab
  text   = '[test] 中文 / émoji / ünïcode'
} | ConvertTo-Json

$bytes = [System.Text.Encoding]::UTF8.GetBytes($body)
Invoke-RestMethod -Uri $url -Method Post -ContentType 'application/json; charset=utf-8' -Body $bytes
```

Three notes on the PowerShell version:

- **Use `Invoke-RestMethod`, not `curl.exe -d`.** PowerShell's single-quoted strings are literal and its native-command argument parsing mangles embedded JSON — the body silently fails to send and you get `411 Length Required`.
- **`-Depth 10` on the `batchUpdate` call is mandatory.** `ConvertTo-Json` defaults to depth 2; that request is nested four levels and the inner objects serialize as the string `System.Collections.Hashtable`.
- **Non-ASCII requires explicit UTF-8 bytes.** Windows PowerShell 5.1 encodes a string body as ISO-8859-1 when the Content-Type carries no charset, turning anything outside ASCII into `?`.

**Verify in the document itself, not just in the return value.** Open the target tab and confirm the text landed there and not in tab one. The return value tells you the call succeeded; only the document tells you it went to the right place.

---

## Step 5 — The MCP server

**5.1** Create a directory and two files.

`package.json`:

```json
{
  "name": "mcp-notebook",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "type": "module",
  "main": "index.js",
  "dependencies": {
    "@modelcontextprotocol/sdk": "^1.0.0"
  }
}
```

`index.js`:

```javascript
#!/usr/bin/env node
/**
 * MCP server — Google Docs via Apps Script web app.
 * Config comes from environment variables set in claude_desktop_config.json:
 *   NOTEBOOK_URL    — the Apps Script /exec URL
 *   NOTEBOOK_SECRET — the shared secret from the script
 */

import { Server } from "@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/index.js";
import { StdioServerTransport } from "@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/stdio.js";
import {
  CallToolRequestSchema,
  ListToolsRequestSchema,
} from "@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/types.js";

const ENDPOINT = process.env.NOTEBOOK_URL;
const SECRET = process.env.NOTEBOOK_SECRET;

if (!ENDPOINT || !SECRET) {
  console.error("Missing NOTEBOOK_URL / NOTEBOOK_SECRET");
  process.exit(1);
}

async function call(op, args = {}) {
  let res;
  try {
    res = await fetch(ENDPOINT, {
      method: "POST",
      headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json; charset=utf-8" },
      body: JSON.stringify({ secret: SECRET, op, ...args }),
    });
  } catch (e) {
    throw new Error(`Request failed: ${e.message}`);
  }

  const text = await res.text();
  let data;
  try {
    data = JSON.parse(text);
  } catch {
    throw new Error(
      `Non-JSON response (HTTP ${res.status}). First 300 chars: ${text.slice(0, 300)}`
    );
  }
  if (!data.ok) throw new Error(data.error || "Unknown error from Apps Script");
  return data;
}

const TOOLS = [
  {
    name: "notebook_list_tabs",
    description:
      "List every tab in the document with its hierarchy depth and tabId. " +
      "Call this before any tab-specific read or write to get accurate tab IDs.",
    inputSchema: { type: "object", properties: {} },
  },
  {
    name: "notebook_read_tab",
    description: "Read the full text content of a specific tab.",
    inputSchema: {
      type: "object",
      properties: {
        tabId: { type: "string", description: "Tab ID, e.g. t.xxxxxxxx" },
      },
      required: ["tabId"],
    },
  },
  {
    name: "notebook_append",
    description:
      "Append plain text to the end of a specific tab, split into paragraphs on newlines. " +
      "Use for log entries, todos, and notes. For heading levels or character styling, " +
      "use notebook_batch_update instead.",
    inputSchema: {
      type: "object",
      properties: {
        tabId: { type: "string", description: "Tab ID, e.g. t.xxxxxxxx" },
        text: { type: "string", description: "Text to append; \\n splits paragraphs" },
      },
      required: ["tabId", "text"],
    },
  },
  {
    name: "notebook_batch_update",
    description:
      "Send raw Google Docs API batchUpdate requests for heading levels, text styles, " +
      "tables, section breaks, and range deletions. Every request's location or " +
      "endOfSegmentLocation MUST carry a tabId or the edit lands in the wrong tab.",
    inputSchema: {
      type: "object",
      properties: {
        requests: {
          type: "array",
          items: { type: "object" },
          description: "Array of Docs API v1 Request objects",
        },
      },
      required: ["requests"],
    },
  },
];

const server = new Server(
  { name: "notebook", version: "1.0.0" },
  { capabilities: { tools: {} } }
);

server.setRequestHandler(ListToolsRequestSchema, async () => ({ tools: TOOLS }));

server.setRequestHandler(CallToolRequestSchema, async (req) => {
  const { name, arguments: args = {} } = req.params;
  try {
    let data;
    switch (name) {
      case "notebook_list_tabs":
        data = await call("listTabs");
        break;
      case "notebook_read_tab":
        data = await call("readTab", { tabId: args.tabId });
        break;
      case "notebook_append":
        data = await call("appendText", { tabId: args.tabId, text: args.text });
        break;
      case "notebook_batch_update":
        data = await call("batchUpdate", { requests: args.requests });
        break;
      default:
        throw new Error(`Unknown tool: ${name}`);
    }
    return { content: [{ type: "text", text: JSON.stringify(data, null, 2) }] };
  } catch (e) {
    return {
      content: [{ type: "text", text: `Error: ${e.message}` }],
      isError: true,
    };
  }
});

const transport = new StdioServerTransport();
await server.connect(transport);
console.error("notebook MCP server started");
```

**5.2** Install and smoke-test:

```bash
npm install
```

```bash
NOTEBOOK_URL='YOUR_WEB_APP_URL' NOTEBOOK_SECRET='YOUR_SECRET' node index.js
```

PowerShell:
```powershell
$env:NOTEBOOK_URL='YOUR_WEB_APP_URL'
$env:NOTEBOOK_SECRET='YOUR_SECRET'
node index.js
```

It should print `notebook MCP server started` and then block waiting on stdin. Ctrl+C to exit. If it prints an error instead, fix that before touching Claude Desktop — a server that can't start on its own will fail silently when the app launches it.

---

## Step 6 — Wire it into Claude Desktop

**6.1 Find the config file.** This is where I lost the most time.

| Install type | Config path |
|---|---|
| macOS | `~/Library/Application Support/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json` |
| Windows (standard installer) | `%APPDATA%\Claude\claude_desktop_config.json` |
| **Windows (Microsoft Store / MSIX)** | `%LOCALAPPDATA%\Packages\Claude_<hash>\LocalCache\Roaming\Claude\claude_desktop_config.json` |

> **The MSIX trap.** If you installed Claude Desktop from the Microsoft Store, it's a packaged app, and packaged apps get `%APPDATA%` redirected into their own sandbox. A PowerShell session is *not* packaged, so its `$env:APPDATA` points at the real `AppData\Roaming` — a completely different directory. Write the config there and Claude Desktop will never see it, Settings will show "No servers added", and the logs directory will be empty because the server was never launched.
>
> You can tell you're on MSIX if you see a folder like `Claude_pzs8sxrjxfjjc` under `%LOCALAPPDATA%\Packages`.
>
> **The reliable way to avoid this entirely:** open Claude Desktop → **Settings → Developer → Edit config**. That button opens the correct file regardless of install type.

**6.2 Write the config:**

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "notebook": {
      "command": "/absolute/path/to/node",
      "args": ["/absolute/path/to/mcp-notebook/index.js"],
      "env": {
        "NOTEBOOK_URL": "YOUR_WEB_APP_URL",
        "NOTEBOOK_SECRET": "YOUR_SECRET"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

macOS / Linux example:

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "notebook": {
      "command": "/usr/local/bin/node",
      "args": ["/Users/YOU/mcp-notebook/index.js"],
      "env": {
        "NOTEBOOK_URL": "https://script.google.com/macros/s/AKfy.../exec",
        "NOTEBOOK_SECRET": "your-generated-secret"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Windows example (note the doubled backslashes — single backslashes are escape characters in JSON):

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "notebook": {
      "command": "C:\\Program Files\\nodejs\\node.exe",
      "args": ["C:\\Users\\YOU\\mcp-notebook\\index.js"],
      "env": {
        "NOTEBOOK_URL": "https://script.google.com/macros/s/AKfy.../exec",
        "NOTEBOOK_SECRET": "your-generated-secret"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

> **Use an absolute path to the node binary, not just `"node"`.** A GUI application inherits the environment it was launched with, which is rarely your shell's environment. This bites hardest on macOS with a version manager — `nvm`, `fnm`, `asdf` and friends set up PATH in your shell profile, which a GUI app never reads, so `"node"` resolves to nothing. It fails quietly.
>
> Find the real path:
> ```bash
> which node          # macOS / Linux
> ```
> ```powershell
> (Get-Command node).Source    # Windows
> ```
> On macOS with nvm this will look something like `/Users/YOU/.nvm/versions/node/v22.3.0/bin/node`. Note that this path changes when you switch Node versions — if the server stops working after an `nvm install`, this is why. A Homebrew install (`/opt/homebrew/bin/node` on Apple Silicon, `/usr/local/bin/node` on Intel) is more stable for this purpose.

**6.3** Quit Claude Desktop **completely** and reopen it.

- **macOS:** Cmd+Q, or Claude → Quit Claude. Closing the window leaves it running.
- **Windows:** right-click the tray icon → Quit. Closing the window leaves it running.
- **Linux:** quit from the application menu; check for a lingering process with `pgrep -fa claude` if the server doesn't appear.

**6.4** Verify in **Settings → Developer**. The server should be listed and running. If it's listed as failed, the error is there, and logs are in the same directory as the config file, under `logs/`.

