## Using it

Ask in natural language:

> List all the tabs in my notebook.

> Read the Decision Log tab.

> Append to the TODO tab: verify the export threshold before freezing the sample.

For anything involving formatting, Claude composes `batchUpdate` requests directly. A heading, for example:

```json
[
  {
    "insertText": {
      "endOfSegmentLocation": { "tabId": "t.xxxxxxxx" },
      "text": "\nMethodology\n"
    }
  },
  {
    "updateParagraphStyle": {
      "range": { "tabId": "t.xxxxxxxx", "startIndex": 0, "endIndex": 12 },
      "paragraphStyle": { "namedStyleType": "HEADING_2" },
      "fields": "namedStyleType"
    }
  }
]
```

One habit worth forming: **tell Claude to read the target tab before writing to it.** A document with established conventions — a particular entry format, a routing notation, a house style — deserves edits that match. Reading first is cheap and it's the difference between a useful collaborator and one that dumps text at the bottom of the page.

