## Gotchas reference

Everything that cost me time, in the order you're likely to hit it. Platform tags mark the ones that only apply to a specific environment.

**`411 Length Required` from a curl call.** *(Windows)*
PowerShell single-quoted strings are literal — backslash is not an escape character — and its native-command argument parsing mangles embedded JSON. The body never gets sent. Use `Invoke-RestMethod` with `ConvertTo-Json` instead of `curl.exe` with `-d`.

**`ConvertTo-Json` produces `System.Collections.Hashtable` inside your payload.** *(Windows)*
It defaults to a depth of 2. A `batchUpdate` request is nested four levels deep. Pass `-Depth 10` or higher.

**Non-ASCII text arrives as `??????`.** *(Windows)*
Windows PowerShell 5.1 encodes a string body as ISO-8859-1 when the Content-Type carries no charset. Convert explicitly:
```powershell
$bytes = [System.Text.Encoding]::UTF8.GetBytes($body)
Invoke-RestMethod -Uri $url -Method Post -ContentType 'application/json; charset=utf-8' -Body $bytes
```
This is a client-side problem only. Node's `fetch` sends UTF-8 natively, so the MCP path is unaffected — but verify it anyway, because if the mangling were happening server-side the whole approach would be unusable for non-English documents.

**The request body contains a literal `$SEC` instead of your secret.** *(macOS / Linux)*
You quoted the heredoc delimiter — `<<'EOF'` suppresses variable expansion. Use `<<EOF`.

**`jq: command not found`.** *(macOS / Linux)*
Only used for pretty-printing. Drop the pipe, or use `| python3 -m json.tool`. `brew install jq` / `apt install jq` if you want it.

**The MCP server worked, then stopped after a Node upgrade.** *(macOS / Linux)*
If `command` points into a version manager's directory (`~/.nvm/versions/node/v22.3.0/bin/node`), that path disappears when you install a new version. Repoint it, or use a Homebrew/system Node whose path is stable.

**Script changes have no effect.**
You saved but didn't redeploy. Deploy → Manage deployments → pencil → Version: New version.

**`Docs is not defined`.**
The Advanced Docs Service isn't enabled. Services → + → Google Docs API.

**Everything writes into the first tab.**
Something called `getBody()` without tab scoping. Use `getTab(tabId).asDocumentTab().getBody()`, or pass `tabId` in the `batchUpdate` location.

**`admin_policy_enforced` during authorization.**
Your Workspace administrator blocks this. Changing project ownership or publishing mode won't help — the policy governs what your account may authorize.

**Claude Desktop shows "No servers added" and the logs directory is empty.**
Wrong config path. On Windows, check whether you're on an MSIX install (see Step 6.1). Use Settings → Developer → Edit config, which opens the correct file on every platform.

**The server is configured but never starts.**
`"command": "node"` isn't resolving in the GUI app's environment. Use the absolute path to the binary — `which node` on macOS/Linux, `(Get-Command node).Source` on Windows. On macOS this is almost always the cause if you use nvm, fnm, or asdf.

**The config looks right but the app still doesn't see the server.**
You closed the window instead of quitting. Cmd+Q on macOS, tray icon → Quit on Windows.

**A GET to the `/exec` URL works in your browser but not from code.**
You're signed into Google in the browser. Test with `curl` from a shell, or a private window, and read the `Location` header of the 302.

