## What I'd do differently

If your account is not in a managed Workspace, the custom OAuth client path (Path A) is more direct: one less hop, no web app to secure, and no shared secret to rotate. The Apps Script approach earns its place specifically when third-party OAuth clients are blocked but self-authored scripts are not — or when you'd rather not maintain OAuth token lifecycle at all.

The thing I got wrong at the start was assuming Apps Script sidesteps organizational policy. It doesn't. It just presents a different app identity, which *may* be treated differently. Test the gate before building anything on top of it.
